## First principles

The generative rules the rest of this file's conventions fall out of live
in [`PRINCIPLES.md`](PRINCIPLES.md). When a decision isn't covered by a
specific convention below, reason from there.

## Secrets

- Credentials live in `~/.key/` (or wherever `keyvault/` is pointed at).
  Read them from there at point of use — never hardcode a secret value or
  echo one into a transcript or commit.
- `~/.key/` starts locked after every reboot on the platforms that support
  encryption-at-rest (`keyvault/README.md`); `_lib/secrets.py` fails with a
  clear locked message rather than silently proceeding, and the freshness
  check flags any job that stalled because of it.

## Git

- Set your account/identity convention here once you have one — which
  account owns these repos, whether they're private, HTTPS vs SSH.
- Commit with explicit identity if the default `git config` on this
  machine isn't yours: `git -c user.name='<you>' -c user.email='<you>'
  commit ...`

## Python

- System/scheduled scripts: the bare system `python3` (no venv) — this is
  why `_lib/` stays stdlib-only; `_lib/selftest.py` enforces it.
- Project tools with real dependencies: give the project its own venv.

## Scheduling

- Job source of truth: `scheduler/manifest.yml`; apply with
  `scheduler/sync.sh` (crontab on Linux, launchd on macOS). Never hand-edit
  crontab or a `.plist` directly — `sync.sh --check` will flag the drift,
  never silently fix it.
- See `scheduler/CONVENTIONS.md` for the found-work-vs-breakage exit
  convention and why jobs must self-notify (neither scheduler pages you by
  default).

## At the start of every session — check for findings

- If **`observability/data/FINDINGS.md`** exists, read it and surface it to
  the operator **before** starting other work. It is written by the daily
  `freshness` job when something is wrong (a job went silent, failed, or
  drifted) and **deleted automatically when everything is clean** — so if the
  file is there, something is currently wrong. Do not treat it as a to-do
  list to action silently; tell the operator what it says.
- **Check its `generated-at` timestamp against the clock.** If it is older
  than about a day, say so plainly: that means *the checker itself has
  stopped running*, which is a bigger problem than anything listed inside the
  file. A monitor that runs under the scheduler cannot report its own death —
  you are the only observer that does not share that fate, which is precisely
  why this instruction exists.
- **Absence of the file is not proof of health.** It means either "nothing
  wrong at the last run" or "the job never ran at all." If you have not seen
  a `freshness` run in `runs.db`, say that rather than reporting all-clear —
  a green report you cannot substantiate is worse than no report.

## Asking another model — the window, never an API key by default

- When you want a second opinion — a review pass, a rival read on a design,
  a one-off "run this by Grok" — go through the window you already have:
  `corral-light consult ask --lane <grok|codex|gemini|claude> --cwd . --prompt "…"`
  (`consult lanes --table` lists what is signed in; `fanout` and `crossfeed`
  are the ⌘↵ and ⇄ buttons from a script). The answer comes back as JSON and
  the pane stays on the wall for the operator to watch, answer, or continue.
- Why: each lane runs the vendor's own CLI under the operator's subscription.
  A vendor API key is a second login, a second bill, and no permission rail;
  reach for one only when the hub is down, and say so in what you write up.
- If Corral Light is not installed on this host, say that plainly instead of
  falling back to an API key on your own — which vendors may see which data
  is the operator's call, not a default.

## Machine fleet (if you run more than one host)

- Name this host here once you have a naming convention. If you run
  several machines, a short shared protocol doc (which host owns which
  capability, where state/heartbeats live) pays off fast — don't build it
  until you actually have a second host.
- One home per capability: don't let the same job run scheduled on two
  hosts "just in case" — pick one, note it here.

